Server option -n: disable password checking (any password is accepted).

The Kindle's root account has a device-specific password that the user does not
know, so -B (allow blank passwords) does not help. With -n, a password login of
an existing user succeeds whatever password the client sends; the user check
(valid_user, -w, -G), -s and -g still apply, and -n is refused together with -t.
Public key logins are unaffected.

Port of KOReader's nopasswd-hack.patch (koreader/koreader-base,
thirdparty/dropbear/nopasswd-hack.patch; same flag and same place in
svr_auth_password), rebased onto dropbear 2026.94, with a help line that says
what it does and a log line at LOG_NOTICE.

diff -ru a/src/runopts.h b/src/runopts.h
--- a/src/runopts.h
+++ b/src/runopts.h
@@ -104,6 +104,7 @@
 	int noauthpass;
 	int norootpass;
 	int allowblankpass;
+	int nopasschk;
 	int multiauthmethod;
 	unsigned int maxauthtries;
 
diff -ru a/src/svr-authpasswd.c b/src/svr-authpasswd.c
--- a/src/svr-authpasswd.c
+++ b/src/svr-authpasswd.c
@@ -80,6 +80,16 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/* -n: accept any password for an existing user. */
+	if (svr_opts.nopasschk) {
+		dropbear_log(LOG_NOTICE,
+				"Password auth succeeded for '%s' from %s (-n: password not checked)",
+				ses.authstate.pw_name,
+				svr_ses.addrstring);
+		send_msg_userauth_success();
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (passwordlen > DROPBEAR_MAX_PASSWORD_LEN) {
 		dropbear_log(LOG_WARNING,
 				"Too-long password attempt for '%s' from %s",
